  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 6,
    "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Time-Domain Impulse",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the continuous-time Fourier transform of a scaled or shifted time-domain impulse by applying the sifting property to obtain the corresponding flat spectrum or complex phase factor.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Time-Domain Impulse",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Fourier Transform of a Time-Domain Impulse",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When the input is a time-domain impulse, the CTFT is handled directly by the sifting property. The integral does not stay as a frequency-dependent expression; it collapses to whatever the exponential evaluates to at the impulse location.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}x(t)e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\delta(t-t_0)f(t)\\,dt=f(t_0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the base case $x(t)=\\delta(t)$. Substituting into the CTFT gives $X(\\omega)=1$, so the spectrum is flat across all frequencies.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\delta(t) \\xleftrightarrow{\\mathcal{F}} 1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The same rule shows what scale and shift do. Multiplying the impulse by $A$ multiplies the flat spectrum by $A$, and shifting the impulse to $t_0$ contributes only the phase factor $e^{-j\\omega t_0}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}A\\,\\delta(t) &\\xleftrightarrow{\\mathcal{F}} A \\\\ \\delta(t-t_0) &\\xleftrightarrow{\\mathcal{F}} e^{-j\\omega t_0} \\\\ A\\,\\delta(t-t_0) &\\xleftrightarrow{\\mathcal{F}} A e^{-j\\omega t_0}\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For this lesson, keep the cue simple: a scaled or shifted impulse never changes the spectrum shape, only its height and phase. That is the transform pair to carry forward into the worked examples.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Time-Domain Impulse",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Transforming the Unit Impulse at the Origin",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the CTFT of $x(t)=\\delta(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the simplest impulse case: the impulse sits at the origin, so the CTFT integral collapses at $t=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\delta(t)e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the sifting property at the origin. The impulse picks out the value of the exponential at $t=0$, so the frequency variable contributes no extra dependence.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=e^{-j\\omega\\cdot 0}=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the unit impulse produces a flat spectrum. In compact transform-pair form, $\\delta(t)\\xleftrightarrow{\\mathcal{F}}1$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Time-Domain Impulse",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Transforming a Scaled Time-Domain Impulse",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the CTFT of $x(t)=3\\delta(t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the same origin-impulse case with one new wrinkle: the impulse is multiplied by $3$. The CTFT integral still collapses at $t=0$, so only the spectrum height changes.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}3\\delta(t)e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Pull the constant $3$ outside the integral and reuse the unit-impulse result from the previous example. The exponential is still evaluated at $t=0$, so there is no frequency dependence to track.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=3\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\delta(t)e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt=3",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So scaling the time-domain impulse scales the flat spectrum by the same factor. In transform-pair form, $3\\delta(t)\\xleftrightarrow{\\mathcal{F}}3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Time-Domain Impulse",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Transforming a Shifted Time-Domain Impulse",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the CTFT of $x(t)=\\delta(t-t_0)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the same impulse-transform pattern again, but now the impulse is shifted to $t_0$. The only change is where the sifting property samples the exponential.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\delta(t-t_0)e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply the sifting property at $t=t_0$. That replaces $t$ in the exponential with the shift location and leaves a pure phase factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=e^{-j\\omega t_0}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So a shifted time-domain impulse transforms to unit magnitude with a frequency-dependent phase: $\\delta(t-t_0)\\xleftrightarrow{\\mathcal{F}}e^{-j\\omega t_0}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Transform of a Time-Domain Impulse",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Transforming a Scaled and Shifted Time-Domain Impulse",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the CTFT of $x(t)=A\\delta(t-t_0)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This combines the two earlier cases: the impulse is shifted to $t_0$ and scaled by $A$. The transform will therefore keep the phase factor from the shift and the amplitude factor from the scale.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}A\\delta(t-t_0)e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Pull the constant $A$ outside the integral, then reuse the shifted-impulse result. The sifting property still evaluates the exponential at $t=t_0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "X(\\omega)=A\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}\\delta(t-t_0)e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt=Ae^{-j\\omega t_0}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the combined effect is just amplitude times phase: $A\\delta(t-t_0)\\xleftrightarrow{\\mathcal{F}}Ae^{-j\\omega t_0}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
